From 2abb01417a04e08a4601b832f489bd28ae8e3629 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 07:53:34 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Gui: the 3D view in stereo, fitted to the person's depth
 limits

Sparky Stereo OS: the 3D view is drawn as a stereo pair from two parallel
cameras that meet at the camera's focal distance: in perspective Coin's own
stereo camera (the eye moved half the separation along the camera's right
vector, the frustum skewed), in orthographic a shear about the focal plane,
set right after the camera as the camera's view volume moved by the inverse
world shear, so the labels and annotations that read the view volume follow
their points.  Coin's stereo camera turns an orthographic volume into a
perspective frustum (the distortion of upstream issues 21496 and 27553), so
it is used for perspective only.

When "Enable 3D" is on, the view's viewport (Quarter's QOpenGLWidget) asks
for stereo buffers in its own format; the edition's Qt shows it as a stereo
window of its own, which Mesa hands to the desktop as full side by side,
while the rest of the window stays one picture.  Quarter draws each frame in
the graphics view's paint event, never in paintGL(), so it draws it there
once per buffer: QOpenGLWidget::makeCurrent(TargetBuffer) aims the scene and
the graphics view's own painting at each eye.

The separation is not a setting.  The person sets the depth limits once, in
the desktop's 3D settings; the view reads them through libstereo-declare
(Wayland: for the top-level window's surface; X11: on the viewport's own
window), four times a second, and a change redraws the view.  Before the left
eye the scene and its annotations are drawn once from the camera between the
eyes into a framebuffer of their own and their depth is read (after the
scene, and after the delayed annotations, which are drawn over a cleared
depth); the separation is the largest under which every pixel stays inside
the limits.  Without limits both eyes are the same picture and the report
view says why, once.  FreeCAD's own eye distance is not offered.

View, Stereo holds "Enable 3D" (the quad-buffer entry, now a check box, the
preference View/Enable3D, on by default, applied to every open 3D view by a
new viewport with the other format) and "3D Display Settings..." (the
desktop's systemsettings kcm_kscreen, else kcmshell6, else told).  The
red/cyan and interleaved views are not offered: the desktop's display
settings make every output from the two eyes.  The Python API's
setStereoType keeps Mono and QuadBuffer for one view and refuses the others
with a sentence; listStereoTypes lists the two.

Picking, preselection and navigation use the camera between the eyes: the
eyes exist only inside the paint event.  The navigation cube, the corner
coordinate system, the background, the rubber band and the task overlay are
drawn in both eyes at the same place, at the screen's depth.  A mouse
selection, which freezes the view as a picture, keeps each eye's own picture.

With FREECAD_STEREO3D_TRACE=1 each frame's fit (limits, k, the focal
distance, the nearest and farthest distances, the separation, the predicted
disparities) and every sentence told are printed on standard error.

Forwarded: not-needed (FreeCAD's AI_POLICY.md and AGENTS.md; the edition's build)
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic)
